# Rules

<!--
  This is this repository's RULES REGISTRY: one entry per rule the project
  holds, each naming the mechanism that ENFORCES it. kh-harness scaffolded
  the shape and will never write a rule here — the rules are yours. It reads
  this file to report two things apart that otherwise look the same:

    a rule Claude is merely TOLD      (enforced-by: none)   — a claim
    a rule something actually HOLDS   (deny/hook/lint/test/critic/ci/review)

  A rule with `enforced-by: none` is a claim; it is the automation backlog.
  kh-harness proposes the cheapest enforcer it can derive for each one.

  FORMAT — one level-2 heading per rule, then `key: value` bullets:

    ## <id> — <the rule, one sentence, imperative>
    - why: <one line: what goes wrong without it>
    - enforced-by: deny | hook | lint | test | critic | ci | review | none
    - pointer: <repository path, or settings:permissions.deny, or settings:hooks.<Event>>
    - verify: <a command a human can run to confirm the enforcer works>
    - priority: high | normal        (optional; `high` rules are re-injected first)
    - status: active | superseded     (optional; superseded rules are kept as a record, not counted)
    - last-verified: YYYY-MM-DD       (optional; when the pointer was last confirmed to work)

  `id` is one token (letters, digits, `.`, `_`, `-`), stable, never reused.
  `pointer` is required for every enforcer except `none` and `review`; a
  pointer that stops resolving is reported — that is the drift signal.

  WORKED EXAMPLE (inside this comment, so it is never loaded as a rule —
  write your own below the comment rather than uncommenting this one):

    ## R1 — Every database query is scoped to the current tenant
    - why: a cross-tenant read is a data breach, not a bug
    - enforced-by: critic
    - pointer: .claude/agents/tenant-scoping.md
    - verify: npm test -- tenant
    - priority: high

  Two things kh-harness installs read this file: `detect` (which reports
  the counts above and any pointer that no longer exists) and, once at
  least one rule exists, the UserPromptSubmit hook `rules-brief.mjs`, which
  puts the rules back in front of Claude on every prompt — bounded, high-
  priority first — because compliance with a rule stated once decays as a
  session runs. Keep each rule to one sentence: the hook has 1500 bytes.
-->
